export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "Matthew",
  display: "Matthew",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Matthew is a fascinating book of the four gospels. I do find it odd that we start the New Testament out with this book though. Out of the four, this first chapter of this gospel doesn't really set a great tone for the rest of the book. People who read through genealogies like this usually get confused, bored, or drained right from the get-go. Because of this, it's hard to recommend a book like Matthew to the average baby Christian. I might recommend John, or Mark for them."},{"kind":"para","text":"That doesn't mean that the book of Matthew is not important. Quite the opposite in fact. Matthew is a book that you must read with a deep sense of understanding in Jesus' character. He is God in the flesh, of course, but Matthew looks primarily at His Words more than at His actions. Mark is a book full of Christ's actions. John takes a look at both (with a real emphasis on Jesus being God in the flesh). Matthew held onto the most important ministry of Jesus, and that was His Words. It's by faith (as we study through Mark) that heals and saves people, not just by miracle."},{"kind":"para","text":"Because Matthew is a book about words, we must pay close attention to the purpose of those words and how it will effect the reader. These are not just here to give us a good sense of Christ talking, or a good sense of the layout of that day. We will see Christ shining light on quite a bit throughout this book, and the things He shines light on are things that He doesn't much care for. He will directly prod and poke the legalism and authoritative abuse in the religion of that day (namely through the Jewish Pharisee's). The same prods and words, however, can be used on much of our religion in our modern age."},{"kind":"para","text":"This really is a book of hope, but it's also a book of ammunition. It helps the readers know what the truth is, and why it's important that we (also) shine light on false teaching, and give people what the real authority is (God's Word, coated in absolute truth). While we will look at the time and age of Jesus as the purpose for His Words, I hope we are able to also look at our own faith, and our own churches, and match up what Jesus is trying to portray through His lessons in this book. They are extremely vital and useful to our faith growth and our practices even still today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Matthew is going to write down what the other disciples seemed to miss the prominence of, and that was the Words of Christ. You will not find the depths of Christ's Words in any of the other three gospels. I think it's very fascinating that there are twelve disciples, four who wrote books, and only one with the viewpoint of paying attention of Jesus' Words. Thankfully He was led by the Holy Spirit to do so, that way we are able to utilize His Word to combat false religion today. Whenever \"Scribes\" or \"Pharisee's\" are mentioned, we must associate them (in our brains) as you would a modern religionist. They look and act the part, but they are not who they say they are."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":1,"title":"The Purpose","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Right off the bat, Matthew deflects his own authority in writing this book."},{"kind":"para","text":"In fact, we get no real reason to even believe that this book is written by Matthew, seeing as he doesn't take credit for it here."},{"kind":"para","text":"We only have a few pieces of evidence that suggest that Matthew is the author."},{"kind":"para","text":"First, we might notice that even in the early church (as manuscripts were being translated, rewritten, and spread around) the scribes that wrote these texts out associated it with the Apostle Matthew (or Levi as we will learn to be his actual name through this book of scripture)."},{"kind":"para","text":"They put Matthew as the source for a reason, and I would assume it's because he personally handed it off to be copied and spread."},{"kind":"para","text":"Second, we have a lot of early scholars of the church (dating back to closer to that time era) who have done far more research than I can afford (like literally, without spending gobs of money to figure out the source of this book, I can't afford it)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Because they have done the majority of the heavy lifting, and have accounts, journals, and evidence far greater than I could ever find, I would rather just believe that they know far more about the subject than I do."},{"kind":"para","text":"Third (if I can even call this a proof), none of the other six Apostles take ownership."},{"kind":"para","text":"I say six because John, and Peter already technically have one."},{"kind":"para","text":"James (the brother of John) was beheaded before it could be written."},{"kind":"para","text":"We don't get much information is regards to the rest of the Apostles ever writing anything else."},{"kind":"para","text":"That just leaves us with Matthew, who seems to have a very important role from his days of doing tax collecting."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Greek (supposedly) written in this original manuscript was at a more scholarly level than the other gospel accounts."},{"kind":"para","text":"I assume Matthews days as a tax collector helped him with that."},{"kind":"para","text":"The other gospel accounts have a lot more aramaic influence in their writing, as that was the traditional language spoken by Jews in that time."},{"kind":"para","text":"Greek was adopted because of the Roman Empire taking over their land."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jews may have learned it to show face, but they (no doubt) hated to use it unless it meant something purposeful (i.e. sharing the gospel with Gentiles)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Either way, the author (in my opinion) doesn't matter to me as much as the purpose."},{"kind":"para","text":"That has always been my take as I've gone through every book of the Bible."},{"kind":"para","text":"I find it fascinating to locate who could continue writing books like 1&2 Samuel when Samuel dies half way through the first book, but I don't let that steal the show."},{"kind":"para","text":"In situations like this, I find it best to find the simplest answer to questions like this, so we can move on from these smaller questions into the larger purpose."},{"kind":"para","text":"Only small-minded people make small questions into main plots, and while an Atheist might use something like the title of a book to compel their arguments, a true scholar will draw from the Text of the book, not the Title."},{"kind":"para","text":"Matthew claims that this book is about the generation of Jesus Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's not about the generation of Matthew."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's not about what Matthew thinks."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's not about why Matthew wrote it."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's not even about if Matthew is the author."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's about Jesus, and that is exactly why Matthew starts this book of with a genealogy."},{"kind":"para","text":"The start of mankind links and connects throughout history to this one man in whom Matthew is going to give a testimony regarding."},{"kind":"para","text":"That man being Jesus the Christ, and the proof of this man's important circles all the way back to the beginning."},{"kind":"para","text":"Except, he doesn't seem to start at Adam..."}]}]},{"start":2,"end":16,"title":"A Faithful Lineage","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, in Old Testament style, I would usually define the names that are given here."},{"kind":"para","text":"For sake of time (and in the spirit of rejecting redundancy on my own part), I am not going to delve into that much in the New Testament."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are nicknames given to people in the New Testament far more often because they lead a life that more attunes to a nickname (rather than the given name they were born with)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Somewhere between the Old Testament and the New Testament, it seems that people stopped naming their children with real purpose."},{"kind":"para","text":"Unless it explicitly says why they are choosing that name, most Jews at this point were just named whatever sounded good, rather than what actually fit the person (or the future hope of that person)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Granted, all of these names we are about to read are Old Testament names in this chapter, but we've already spent a fair amount of time going through them in the Old Testament, so there is no real reason to continue defining them (unless it's a necessity for the main theme of the book)."},{"kind":"para","text":"What I find strange, here, is that Matthew starts with Abraham."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's on purpose though."},{"kind":"para","text":"Matthew is appealing to the religionists (the Judaisers as Paul might call them)."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Scribes and Pharisee's of that day were people who knew their Bible extremely well."},{"kind":"para","text":"Their religious system stems from putting Abraham and Moses as main figures and purposes for their faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps that is why their religious system was so flawed."},{"kind":"para","text":"They put men as their authority rather than God."},{"kind":"para","text":"Over and over again in the Old Testament, God warns against doing this."},{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham and Moses (by the way) were extremely flawed individuals."},{"kind":"para","text":"We read about the many sins each of them faced in their own flesh and will, and God was not happy with some of their choices."},{"kind":"para","text":"Yet, the religious (even still today) look to them as the real authority of their faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"If your faith rests upon a dead man, you certainly have a dead faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"Some might say, \"Okay, so that means your faith is dead too, because Jesus died!\""},{"kind":"para","text":"You're right!"},{"kind":"para","text":"But as we will learn, Jesus didn't stay dead."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's a spoiler though (even though everyone (whether they believe or not) should already know this)."},{"kind":"para","text":"A faith that rests in Jesus is a faith that has been resurrected."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's a new faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"Not one passed down from traditions and works of our fathers."},{"kind":"para","text":"One that Jesus brings us to without any past strings attached."},{"kind":"para","text":"In fact, Jesus is the best place to go for people who have struggled with past sins and traditions."},{"kind":"para","text":"He cuts you free from all of it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Here's what I will do, though, as we go through these names."},{"kind":"para","text":"Counting is important to Matthew, so I say we count with him."},{"kind":"para","text":"As a tax-collector, he MUST know how to count, and he will give a count at the end of this chapter."},{"kind":"para","text":"But let's just double check with him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Right here, we have four generations (Abraham to Judah)."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here we have three more generations (Judah to Aram)."},{"kind":"para","text":"By the way, this can be followed throughout the Old Testament as well."},{"kind":"para","text":"Granted it would take far more time than to just read them labelled out here, but these are lineages you certainly can take the time to follow if you haven't yet."},{"kind":"para","text":"You will get into the deeper purposes of these people too, and what has ultimately led us to this chapter."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is why (I believe) Matthew starts this chapter off like this."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Old Testament should not just be detached from the New Testament."},{"kind":"para","text":"Matthew directly connects them here by labelling out the important people leading up to the Savior."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have three more generations to account for here (Aram to Salmon)."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I want us to take a moment of pause here as we read this because I find it interesting."},{"kind":"para","text":"We might have noticed throughout the Old Testament that women aren't usually spoken about very often."},{"kind":"para","text":"Yet, here in Matthew, he is sure to mention key women characters of faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think a lot of women in the Old Testament are not mentioned too much because they followed their husbands advice and authority very closely (as was custom in that culture in those days)."},{"kind":"para","text":"If there were more women like Ruth, or like Rahab (who is also mentioned here (Rachab is Rahab)), there would be a lot more women spoken about in scripture making a large impact for faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"That was not the case, however, and so many of the genealogies are focused on men."},{"kind":"para","text":"But we do get glimpses of strong faith-filled women every so often that should give both genders hope for their own faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have three more generations mentioned here (Salmon to Jesse)."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, in this verse, we have a mention of a woman, but she was not a woman who implored the faith very well."},{"kind":"para","text":"She was a cheater to her husband Urias (or Uriah rather) and so Matthew doesn't think it's fit to write her name here."},{"kind":"para","text":"She did give birth, however, to Solomon, and that is important to note because he was an integral part of this lineage, the growth of Israel, and a few books of the Bible that are extremely useful still today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Here we have two generations."},{"kind":"para","text":"Let's add up what we have so far so we don't lose count:"},{"kind":"para","text":"From Abraham to Solomon is fifteen generations."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"From Rehoboam (or Roboam as it's written here) we get the division of Israel."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is when we can look through the account of Jesus' lineage through the eyes of Luke:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Jesus himself began to be about thirty years of age, being (as was supposed) the son of Joseph, which was the son of Heli, Which was the son of Matthat, which was the son of Levi, which was the son of Melchi, which was the son of Janna, which was the son of Joseph, Which was the son of Mattathias, which was the son of Amos, which was the son of Naum, which was the son of Esli, which was the son of Nagge, Which was the son of Maath, which was the son of Mattathias, which was the son of Semei, which was the son of Joseph, which was the son of Juda, Which was the son of Joanna, which was the son of Rhesa, which was the son of Zorobabel, which was the son of Salathiel, which was the son of Neri, Which was the son of Melchi, which was the son of Addi, which was the son of Cosam, which was the son of Elmodam, which was the son of Er, Which was the son of Jose, which was the son of Eliezer, which was the son of Jorim, which was the son of Matthat, which was the son of Levi, Which was the son of Simeon, which was the son of Juda, which was the son of Joseph, which was the son of Jonan, which was the son of Eliakim, Which was the son of Melea, which was the son of Menan, which was the son of Mattatha, which was the son of Nathan, which was the son of David, Which was the son of Jesse, which was the son of Obed, which was the son of Booz, which was the son of Salmon, which was the son of Naasson, Which was the son of Aminadab, which was the son of Aram, which was the son of Esrom, which was the son of Phares, which was the son of Juda, Which was the son of Jacob, which was the son of Isaac, which was the son of Abraham, which was the son of Thara, which was the son of Nachor, Which was the son of Saruch, which was the son of Ragau, which was the son of Phalec, which was the son of Heber, which was the son of Sala, Which was the son of Cainan, which was the son of Arphaxad, which was the son of Sem, which was the son of Noe, which was the son of Lamech, Which was the son of Mathusala, which was the son of Enoch, which was the son of Jared, which was the son of Maleleel, which was the son of Cainan, Which was the son of Enos, which was the son of Seth, which was the son of Adam, which was the son of God.","ref":"Luke 3:23-38"},{"kind":"para","text":"If you notice, Solomon is not mentioned here."},{"kind":"para","text":"Rather Nathan is the lineage that Luke follows."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's on purpose."},{"kind":"para","text":"Luke also goes all the way back to Adam rather than stopping at Abraham."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is argued that Matthews account follows the lineage of faith, while Luke's account follows the lineage of humanity."},{"kind":"para","text":"But we can study that more when we get to the book of Luke."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have three generations (from Solomon to Asa)."}]},{"start":8,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Once we get to Babylon, we don't have too much information in regards to these lineages."},{"kind":"para","text":"I'm sure we could find some hints of things throughout the last part of the Old Testament, but it starts to get a little more difficult to track."},{"kind":"para","text":"I trust that Matthew's retelling is accurate though, specifically because everything else was accurate."},{"kind":"para","text":"From Asa to Jechonias is ten generations."},{"kind":"para","text":"So now we have a total of twenty-eight generations from Abraham to Jechonias (or the carrying away to Babylon)."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think it should also be noted that Babylon carried away the southern kingdom (namely Judah)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Assyria carried away the Northern kingdom (the rest of the tribes)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Matthew is not speaking about the Assyrian Captivity at all as we can see here."},{"kind":"para","text":"He is only focusing on Judah."},{"kind":"para","text":"Again, I believe this to be for a reason."},{"kind":"para","text":"A promise is given to Judah by Jacob in Genesis:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Judah, thou art he whom thy brethren shall praise: thy hand shall be in the neck of thine enemies; thy father's children shall bow down before thee. Judah is a lion's whelp: from the prey, my son, thou art gone up: he stooped down, he couched as a lion, and as an old lion; who shall rouse him up? The sceptre shall not depart from Judah, nor a lawgiver from between his feet, until Shiloh come; and unto him shall the gathering of the people be. Binding his foal unto the vine, and his ass's colt unto the choice vine; he washed his garments in wine, and his clothes in the blood of grapes: His eyes shall be red with wine, and his teeth white with milk.","ref":"Genesis 49:8-12"},{"kind":"para","text":"That promise is kept through Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"That will be obvious as we continue to read."},{"kind":"para","text":"Here is two more generations (from Jechonias to Zorobabel)."}]},{"start":13,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we finish up the generations by adding Zorobabel to Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is eleven more generations here."},{"kind":"para","text":"We now have a count of forty-one generations in total."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you need to, you may go back and recount them as well."},{"kind":"para","text":"Maybe even highlight the names of the men throughout each generation."},{"kind":"para","text":"I'm putting an emphasis on this for a reason as we will see in a moment..."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"title":"Math Class","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"According to this math, Abraham to Christ is forty-two generations (14x3 is forty-two)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Yet that is not the answer we got while counting along with Matthew."},{"kind":"para","text":"From Abraham to David IS fourteen generations."},{"kind":"para","text":"He is right, and you can go back and count to make sure."},{"kind":"para","text":"From David to Babylon (or Jechonias) is fourteen generations."},{"kind":"para","text":"That, again, is accurate and correct."},{"kind":"para","text":"But from Jechonias (or Babylon) to Christ is thirteen generations."},{"kind":"para","text":"Go ahead and recount from Babylon to Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is not fourteen generations."},{"kind":"para","text":"Either Matthew was not a good tax-collector, or something is very wrong here."},{"kind":"para","text":"So what's the issue?"},{"kind":"para","text":"I have done as much research as humanly possible to figure out what the error is in this text, and I have come back with differing viewpoints."},{"kind":"para","text":"Some viewpoints count Mary, here, as a part of that stating that Mary's father was also named Joseph and there was some sort of confusion while translating this part of scripture because of it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps that is true."},{"kind":"para","text":"But the translation is actually accurate."},{"kind":"para","text":"In the Greek, it is worded like this as well \"Jacob begat Joseph the HUSBAND of Mary...\""},{"kind":"para","text":"Husband means what it has always meant, and that was that she was married to this Joseph."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are some who believe that Matthew likely just skipped names in the lineage to try and match that magic \"14\" number, but it still wouldn't make sense (because there are plenty of names that he skips, which would extend the lists beyond 14 each one)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Others believe that Matthew wrote this list to reference some sort of symbolism in the name of David directly linking Jesus to David (I don't hold to this)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps the missing individual is actually a no-brainer in the list."},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps the fourteenth father to beget Jesus was not a human that could be listed."},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps the purpose is to place emphasis on the fact that it is God (the Father) who begat Jesus Christ through Mary, and that is why it's written this way."},{"kind":"para","text":"There's no way any Bible writer (led by the Holy Spirit) missed this or messed this up on purpose."},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps it is a translation error somewhere (though I highly doubt it)."},{"kind":"para","text":"I find it far more intriguing to think that Matthew is giving the last generation over to God specifically, knowing that Joseph (Mary's husband) is not the real father of Jesus Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"Yet Joseph is still linked to promised generation, and through adoption of Jesus, he fits the promise of the Messiah."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you don't like this generation account, because it leads to Joseph (who is not biologically related to Jesus), then you might prefer Luke's account (which goes straight to Mary who IS directly related to Jesus)."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will also see Jesus refer to God as \"The Father\" over and over again throughout Matthew reminding the reader that God is not just \"The Creator\"."},{"kind":"para","text":"There's a lot of depth to Jesus calling God \"The Father\"."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let your light so shine before men, that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father which is in heaven.","ref":"Matthew 5:16"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That ye may be the children of your Father which is in heaven: for he maketh his sun to rise on the evil and on the good, and sendeth rain on the just and on the unjust.","ref":"Matthew 5:45"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That thine alms may be in secret: and thy Father which seeth in secret himself shall reward thee openly.","ref":"Matthew 6:4"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If ye then, being evil, know how to give good gifts unto your children, how much more shall your Father which is in heaven give good things to them that ask him?","ref":"Matthew 7:11"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Whosoever therefore shall confess me before men, him will I confess also before my Father which is in heaven. But whosoever shall deny me before men, him will I also deny before my Father which is in heaven.","ref":"Matthew 10:32-33"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"At that time Jesus answered and said, I thank thee, O Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because thou hast hid these things from the wise and prudent, and hast revealed them unto babes. Even so, Father: for so it seemed good in thy sight. All things are delivered unto me of my Father: and no man knoweth the Son, but the Father; neither knoweth any man the Father, save the Son, and he to whomsoever the Son will reveal him.","ref":"Matthew 11:25-27"},{"kind":"para","text":"etc."},{"kind":"para","text":"Over and over again we will see Jesus refer to God as \"The Father\" and I believe that is vital and important as we study along through this book."},{"kind":"para","text":"Either way, Jesus links to David one way or another, and there was no way for the Pharisee's of Matthews day, nor the Pharisee's of our day to deny that Jesus comes through royal faith blood in Israel."},{"kind":"para","text":"Thankfully, Matthew doesn't just leave this chapter off here with an inaccurate count."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe he explains the story of Joseph here to demonstrate why he chose to count the lineage this way:"}]}]},{"start":18,"end":25,"title":"Joseph's Vision","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are revealed quite a few truths here."},{"kind":"para","text":"First, we recognize that Joseph and Mary were not married."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were engaged to get married, but they had not had the wedding yet."},{"kind":"para","text":"Before they were able to \"come together\" (or rather before they were able \"cohabitate\" i.e. have other children, get married, and live together) Mary was already pregnant."},{"kind":"para","text":"Worse for Joseph, it wasn't his baby."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were never intimate with each other, and here she is with someone elses baby."},{"kind":"para","text":"Matthew claims the baby was given to her by \"the Holy Ghost\" but Joseph has a hard time believing this."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's a lot more in depth if you read the book of Luke, but Matthew is getting this picture here so we understand that the one who begat Jesus was not Joseph."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph was a \"just\" man."},{"kind":"para","text":"Justified, perhaps."},{"kind":"para","text":"Being honorary."},{"kind":"para","text":"Trying to make things appeal outwardly as righteous and good."},{"kind":"para","text":"He did not want to be accused by his family and others in the Jewish faith as an adulterous man, so he was going to \"put her away\"."},{"kind":"para","text":"Again, they are not married yet, so technically Joseph didn't have permanent ties to Mary at this particular point."},{"kind":"para","text":"Supposedly the law of Moses was serious about this matter of marriage."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you were engaged to a woman it was almost equivalent to being married to that woman (you just didn't have the ceremony yet)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph (as well as the city) had the right to stone Mary for being with a child outside of her engagement."},{"kind":"para","text":"But Joseph loved her too much to make her a \"public example\"."},{"kind":"para","text":"Mary will be better understood and looked at in Luke, but Matthew wants us to pay attention to the type of man Joseph is."},{"kind":"para","text":"He is a good man, who wants the best for his soon to be wife, and he wants to make sure that she is not ridiculed and mistreated."},{"kind":"para","text":"Even though he certainly could have gotten even with her (assuming she was unfaithful, a person doesn't just \"become\" pregnant."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's something that has to be initiated), he wants to also believe that what she is saying about this child is real."},{"kind":"para","text":"His only real solution to this problem was to try and get divorced in secret."},{"kind":"para","text":"Thankfully he takes a night to think about it, and thankfully, God intervenes:"}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know what it is about Joseph's and dreams in scripture, but it seems that God likes to reveal Himself and give bold acclamations to them in dream states."},{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, in this verse, we have a direct reference to David."},{"kind":"para","text":"The angel is calling Joseph the \"son of David\" because Joseph comes through that family line."},{"kind":"para","text":"The angel assures Joseph that it is the Holy Ghost that gives this child to Mary."},{"kind":"para","text":"She was not unfaithful, she was recieving a promise:"}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jesus\" means \"Savior.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus will save His people from their sins."},{"kind":"para","text":"How He does that is through a perfect life lived before God, and dying as a sacrifice for those that believe in Him AS the sacrifice of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"These are promises given to Isreal for generations now."},{"kind":"para","text":"Isaiah is full of these promises being made, and I believe Joseph likely knew what the promises were."},{"kind":"para","text":"He probably didn't think that it would be through his own son though."}]},{"start":22,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This prophecy is from Isaiah."},{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph was going to have to trust the Lord on this one, or else he will not be a part of the promise that all of Israel had been waiting for for so long."},{"kind":"para","text":"Imagine if Joseph decided he wouldn't be involved with this."},{"kind":"para","text":"What a sad tale of Jesus' father..."},{"kind":"para","text":"But Joseph (as we will see) listens."},{"kind":"para","text":"God did not choose this family by accident."},{"kind":"para","text":"I know a lot of emphasis is placed on Mary as the chosen mother of Christ, and I have nothing against it, but we must also understand and know that Joseph was also chosen by God."},{"kind":"para","text":"It was not by accident that Joseph was in this relationship."},{"kind":"para","text":"It wasn't by accident that she was pregnant before they officially married."},{"kind":"para","text":"It won't be by accident that people will try and shame this family for being so poor, or being so unorganized, or not being religious enough."},{"kind":"para","text":"Even through the birth of Christ He was already breaking tradition and religious systems set in place by (perhaps) well-intentioned men of the law."}]},{"start":24,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Knew her not\" meant that he was not intimate with her until after Jesus was born."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus has other family members."},{"kind":"para","text":"He has brothers and sisters through Joseph and Mary, but Jesus was the only one born before Joseph made any passes on Mary."},{"kind":"para","text":"Either way, Joseph stepped up, though the cards were stacked against him, and God uses that testimony in many ways."},{"kind":"para","text":"One way is through the loving adoption of God to usward."},{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph gave us an example of how we can be accepted into the promise without being related."},{"kind":"para","text":"We must simply believe and obey the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Another way is that God joins people together, and it is not by accident."},{"kind":"para","text":"If people split apart there is only one of two reasons for it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Either one person or the other doesn't have the Lord in marriage, or both people allowed a schism that caused man to break the marriage apart."},{"kind":"para","text":"Whichever is the case, it's usually not a good sign to see divorce in a couple who have been given to each other under the direct commandment and gift of God."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Thou knowest the commandments, Do not commit adultery, Do not kill, Do not steal, Do not bear false witness, Defraud not, Honour thy father and mother.","ref":"Mark 10:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"The fact is, Joseph was not Jesus' blood father, but Joseph was still going to raise this child as if it were his own."},{"kind":"para","text":"He was going to love Jesus, and he was going to help Jesus grow to become the man that God wants Him to be."},{"kind":"para","text":"Or perhaps it was opposite."},{"kind":"para","text":"Maybe Jesus helped Joseph become the man HE was supposed to be."},{"kind":"para","text":"Whatever the case may be, God chose this man, and this lineage, for the sole purpose of bringing in the promises that He has made for generations, and it was time for Jesus to prove those promises, not just through His actions, but through His Word."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":25}]},
    "2": {"chapter":2,"intro":[],"previously":["In our last chapter, Matthew opens this book up with the obvious focal of Jesus Christ. This is a gospel account, but it's only a gospel account because Jesus is the main theme. Without Him, the gospel would not be \"good\" news. Matthew opened up to the lineage of Jesus, talked about how Joseph dealt with the idea of his soon to be wife's pregnancy, the dream that ensues to give him clarity to continue with the marriage, and the happy circumstance of a miracle (both in the fact that there was a baby going to be born, and the fact that she was a virgin who was pregnant).","In this chapter, we get the power of Jesus right at His birth. He is already shining light on evil in this world. Israel was a dark place at this time, and the obvious fact that the King starts to panic proves that Jesus is the light of the world. The darkness hates light. When a man is controlled by darkness, he will do anything and everything in his power to snuff out light (especially if that light is showing people the truth of the evil in a persons heart)."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"King of the Jews","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Matthew is affirmative on his facts here."},{"kind":"para","text":"He isn't going to try and prove that Jesus was born in Bethlehem, he is stating that it is a matter of fact."},{"kind":"para","text":"We would probably guess, as well, that Matthew would be in his thirties when he starts to follow Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"They are close to the same age."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's important to note, because Herod is not a good guy (as we will soon see in a moment)."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Another thing to note (as, once again, a matter of fact) is that these \"wise men\" were searching for the \"King of the Jews\"."},{"kind":"para","text":"Where do they get this idea and title for Him?"},{"kind":"para","text":"It's honestly a great mystery."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't assume they were just going on random chance or theory."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were \"wise\" men."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Wise men\" means \"astrologers\" who, having discovered by the rising of a remarkable star that the Messiah had just been born."},{"kind":"para","text":"It can also mean \"a false prophet and sorcerer.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"They were names given by the Babylonians referring to \"teachers, priests, physicians, astrologers, seers, interpreters of dreams, augers, soothsayers, sorcerers, etc.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"They were book smart."},{"kind":"para","text":"But how did they know that the star led to the \"King of the Jews\"?"},{"kind":"para","text":"How did they know that heading in that direction was going to lead them to a proper place."},{"kind":"para","text":"Well, actually, the star led them to an improper person."},{"kind":"para","text":"Herod is a sick man (mentally)."},{"kind":"para","text":"He is hearing about a new competition for the first time."},{"kind":"para","text":"These \"wise\" men were doing something extremely dangerous in this moment, but I think it's going to be a useful story for God's providence."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's going to be a theme in Matthew."},{"kind":"para","text":"Evil leaders doing evil things, and Jesus constantly shining light on it and rejecting it."},{"kind":"para","text":"But why a star?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Well, my only guess is from the prophecy of Balaam."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"","ref":"Numbers 24:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously Balaam was not a good guy, but he was used by God to provide some truth."},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps they didn't know any particular prophecy, but they are going based on some sort of prompting by God."},{"kind":"para","text":"During this time in the history of Israel, they are not a prosperous nation."},{"kind":"para","text":"They aren't looked at as anything special anymore."},{"kind":"para","text":"They had experienced two MASS captivities that left their land in desolation."},{"kind":"para","text":"While some of it has been built back up, they are still owned by the enemy, and that continues even during the time of Christ's ministry."},{"kind":"para","text":"They are no longer owned by the Babylonians, they are owned by Rome, and this place that the \"wise\" men come to, is not much of a place of prosperity."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is a rare thing to get an audience with a king."},{"kind":"para","text":"Yet, here they are, as strangers in a strange land standing before the king of the land."},{"kind":"para","text":"This likely means that either the king doesn't have all that much power, or the wise men were somehow led (prophetically) to the king for a purpose."},{"kind":"para","text":"The part that is funny to me is that the wise men are learning that this is all news to the people of Jerusalem."},{"kind":"para","text":"No one else was nearly as excited about finding the King as much as these men were."},{"kind":"para","text":"In fact, they are literally standing in front of a king at that very moment, and they don't seem to be excited about meeting him at all."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's because this king (Herod) will not give his life to save humanity."},{"kind":"para","text":"In fact, as we will see in a moment, he will be giving babies lives to try and please his own agenda..."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":7,"title":"A Worry of Evil Authority","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Wise Men were excited, but the king (and Jerusalem apparently) were troubled."},{"kind":"para","text":"Herod was troubled because there was now some sort of competition to his authority."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's how you know someone is evil."},{"kind":"para","text":"If they feel like they have to fight and fend for their position and title, they are likely not led by the Lord at all."},{"kind":"para","text":"The only authority in this world is the authority of truth (which is found ultimately in scripture)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus is the truth, and because of that (alone) we are assured of His authority."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is why He will eventually have thousands following Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Not because He claimed Himself as King, and not because He made Himself as the leader, but simply because He will speak the truth (as Matthew will point out very vividly throughout this book of scripture)."},{"kind":"para","text":"While that certainly matters, it is not the main ministry of Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter (Mark rather) thought the main ministry of Jesus was His healing and actions."},{"kind":"para","text":"His main ministry was not His actions (like Mark wrote)."},{"kind":"para","text":"His main ministry was not in the details of His life (like Luke wrote)."},{"kind":"para","text":"His main ministry was not in the things He said (like Matthew wrote)."},{"kind":"para","text":"His main ministry was connecting God to man (without the rules of religion) (like John wrote)."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will see (through the sermons and speeches of Jesus) that He will constantly speak about this."},{"kind":"para","text":"As a little baby, He is already shining light on evil people, and that is why people are upset about this news rather than excited about Salvation."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were afraid of an overthrow, when they should have been excited for a new life."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were especially afraid because the wise men came \"to worship him\" according to verse 2."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The chief priests and scribes will become the main tools throughout Jesus' ministry to try and silence Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"They will not succeed."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus will say everything He needs to, and a lot of people will be set free from the yoke and bonds of the Pharisee's religious rule because Jesus will preach against it and prove why the way they do things are wrong."},{"kind":"para","text":"The funny part is, we read this prophecy from Numbers and (as a unlearned studier of scripture) I would have never connected the two things together."},{"kind":"para","text":"So for Herod to get scribes and chief priests to answer this question must have been a bit humorous."},{"kind":"para","text":"They likely thought, \"How could they possibly know that this star leads to the 'King of the Jews'?\""},{"kind":"para","text":"They would have to do some deeper studying, and they will only find it in one place:"}]},{"start":5,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They don't even quote Numbers."},{"kind":"para","text":"They don't use their most prized prophet whom they claim to adore."},{"kind":"para","text":"The words and law of Moses held a very high position, and seeing as Numbers is written by Moses, you'd think they would have known the prophecy far better."},{"kind":"para","text":"Instead, they use a different prophet:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"","ref":"Micah 5:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"But even if they didn't remember what Micah 5:2 says, they could get the same information about the promised Messiah throughout all of the Old Testament scriptures."},{"kind":"para","text":"Everything leads to Jesus and this moment in world history."},{"kind":"para","text":"It all leads to the birth of God's promise for salvation to the world."},{"kind":"para","text":"Whatever the reason is, they bring up Micah, and that is enough for Herod to panic."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The keyword here is \"privily\"."},{"kind":"para","text":"Herod wanted this to stay quiet."},{"kind":"para","text":"He didn't want anyone else to know about this potential \"King\" these men were speaking of."},{"kind":"para","text":"He wanted to try and find the child himself, and follow the same star they were following."},{"kind":"para","text":"Knowing the \"time\" the star appeared, would help him know what direction he should be facing to go after it, potentially even getting to the child before they do if the star hasn't been there long."},{"kind":"para","text":"There's no real answer to how long it took the Magi to get to Christ, but some scholars believe it could have taken them about two years at this point."},{"kind":"para","text":"If that's true, Herod would be out of luck finding Jesus at this point."},{"kind":"para","text":"But the wise men will be led a different way."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":10,"title":"Herod's Lie","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He let the Wise Men go to Bethlehem (where they assumed the child would be according to scripture), and Herod claims that he (too) wants to worship the child."},{"kind":"para","text":"Let's know the facts though; Herod didn't want to worship Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"Herod was afraid of Jesus because it would mean he might potentially lose his power and authority."},{"kind":"para","text":"He wants to be the king, and a small child can't do this to him."},{"kind":"para","text":"We must be reminded that he is intimidated by an unknown promise, and a (possibly) two-year-old child."},{"kind":"para","text":"I've seen pastors and church leaders who are so prompt about their title and position that they are willing to hurt and damage the youngest of the congregation to keep their place."},{"kind":"para","text":"In fact, a pastor had mistreated my own daughter (eleven-years-old) because of his own insecurities, even telling her to leave the church."},{"kind":"para","text":"Herod wanted authority more than salvation, and there are many church leaders who think the same way."},{"kind":"para","text":"As we will see, there are many casualties to an evil leader looking to try and stay in power with everything that they have."}]},{"start":9,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I'm assuming it has been some time since they saw that star."},{"kind":"para","text":"If they had travelled two years from the initial viewing of the star, they might have thought they were wasting their time seeing as Herod (the king of this land) didn't even know or care about this."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now that the star reappears, it reaffirms their work and progress."},{"kind":"para","text":"They become excited about it again."},{"kind":"para","text":"They weren't just \"happy\", this says they \"rejoiced with EXCEEDING GREAT joy.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"This was a thrill and awe to them."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were amazed that they were right about the prophecy."},{"kind":"para","text":"They are sure to find the King."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":12,"title":"The Gift of God","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, we get a little more information about the birth of Christ in the book of Luke."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"","ref":"Luke 2:4-7"},{"kind":"para","text":"A lot of people are under the assumption that Mary and Joseph were poor parents."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know if I believe that."},{"kind":"para","text":"They just couldn't get a hotel room."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were willing to pay for the room, but there was \"no room for them in the inn\" so instead they had to sleep with the animals because she was about to deliver her baby."},{"kind":"para","text":"I wouldn't assume they were rich, but they were certainly not \"poor\" by the standards of which poor homeless families might have been in those days."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were even going to pay their taxes like they were supposed to."},{"kind":"para","text":"They had the money for it, and they were travelling to do it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps coming from Nazareth is associated with being poor, I don't really know."},{"kind":"para","text":"Either way, the Wise Men find the child, and the gifts that are presented will certainly give this family quite a bit of money."},{"kind":"para","text":"Notice that they find the family in a \"house\"."},{"kind":"para","text":"It was not in a stable that the wise men are presenting their gifts in like some would have you to believe."},{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, this would symbolize that they weren't exactly \"poor\"."},{"kind":"para","text":"Can we note as well that they didn't see this family and worship Mary."},{"kind":"para","text":"They worshipped \"him\"."},{"kind":"para","text":"They worshipped the baby."},{"kind":"para","text":"They brought JESUS the gifts, not Mary."},{"kind":"para","text":"Normally (at baby showers), we bring gifts for the baby, but they are given for the sake of the mother."},{"kind":"para","text":"These gifts were likely used by the whole family, but they were specifically for Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were gifts in HONOR of Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"The gifts signify a lot."},{"kind":"para","text":"They are not just random gifts picked up at the last minute."},{"kind":"para","text":"These were valuable and they all represented a part of the promise."},{"kind":"para","text":"Gold represents His birth and the value that His presence on Earth brings."},{"kind":"para","text":"There's nothing more valuable (even still today) than gold."},{"kind":"para","text":"Frankincense represents the value of His life."},{"kind":"para","text":"Often it was used for fragrance in worshipping God."},{"kind":"para","text":"That speaks volumes about Jesus alone."},{"kind":"para","text":"Then the Myrrh represents the value of His death."},{"kind":"para","text":"Myrrh in those days was used as anointing oil, specifically in embalming bodies for burial."},{"kind":"para","text":"Throughout scripture, anytime we see these three substances mentioned, they all link to those three truths of Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"We should have the view of that whenever they pop up."},{"kind":"para","text":"If gold is mentioned, our brains must automatically link it to the value of Jesus' presence."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is nothing more valuable than Jesus."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"","ref":"1 Peter 1:7-8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"","ref":"Hebrews 11:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"It's not the gifts themselves that matter."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's what they represented."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the only reason that we are told about these three particular gifts."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe there were likely more than three wise men in this house at this time."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe there were a lot more gifts given too."},{"kind":"para","text":"But we are only told about three for a reason, and that reason is the significance of Christ's life compared to the full story of scripture."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I'm thankful that God warned these men."},{"kind":"para","text":"They would have likely been killed, and Jesus would have been direct fire under Herod at that point."},{"kind":"para","text":"So they leave a different way, that way they don't chance bringing harm to their King."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, that sparks a different kind of problem:"}]}]},{"start":13,"end":15,"title":"Out of Egypt","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, Joseph has a dream about this boy."},{"kind":"para","text":"So far it has been accurate about who Jesus is, so why stop listening to the angel of the Lord now?"},{"kind":"para","text":"I find \"destroy\" to be an interesting word here."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Destroy\" means \"to put out of the way entirely, abolish, put an end to ruin.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, I think Herod wouldn't have just simply \"killed\" Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"He likely would have tortured the child to death."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is a sad thing to think of, but it's a very common reality of our sinful world."},{"kind":"para","text":"Today, our country has no problem killing and torturing a child in the womb."},{"kind":"para","text":"They don't just kill it quickly and painlessly."},{"kind":"para","text":"They rip it apart piece by piece while it's still alive."},{"kind":"para","text":"If they had the ability, they might even do it to a two year old these days."},{"kind":"para","text":"So when we read a story like this and it uses a term like \"destroy\" towards a child, the imagination can go wild and it's still not wild enough."},{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph must be smart and listen to the prompting of the Lord here."},{"kind":"para","text":"He will not like the repercussions if he doesn't."}]},{"start":14,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"One prophet says \"Born in Bethlehem\" another prophet says \"out of Egypt\" and another prophet says \"He will be called a Nazarite.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"All the prophecies are also true."},{"kind":"para","text":"The prophet that claims this verse is Hosea."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"","ref":"Hosea 11:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph did what he was told."},{"kind":"para","text":"He stayed in Egypt until the Lord said to move again."},{"kind":"para","text":"How long that was, I don't know, but Joseph and Mary were going to have to trust what God told them until it was time to listen to what God told them next."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":18,"title":"A Serial Killer King","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a terrible verse."},{"kind":"para","text":"He chooses to kill any child under his authority two and under."},{"kind":"para","text":"That had to be a lot of children ripped from their homes and murdered in front of their family."},{"kind":"para","text":"Notice that he is not killing the children to prevent a problem."},{"kind":"para","text":"He is killing the children because he felt powerless."},{"kind":"para","text":"Evil men will go to extremes because of something they are hiding."},{"kind":"para","text":"The fact is, Herod is hiding something heinous."},{"kind":"para","text":"It was more than just wanting power."},{"kind":"para","text":"It was wanting to keep something he loved dearly."},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps it was wealth."},{"kind":"para","text":"Maybe it was women."},{"kind":"para","text":"Maybe it was material possession or something that is so dark that he didn't want coming to light."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know what it is, and I don't really care."},{"kind":"para","text":"A person that is able to kill children because of his own insecurities (at least in my book) doesn't deserve to explain himself."},{"kind":"para","text":"He deserves death."},{"kind":"para","text":"Period."}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The sad reality is, God knew this would come to pass."},{"kind":"para","text":"He warns about it in Jeremiah."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"","ref":"Jeremiah 31:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"Ramah is at the opposite of Jerusalem from Bethlehem."},{"kind":"para","text":"Bethlehem is in the south of Jerusalem, and Ramah is in the northern most part of Jerusalem."},{"kind":"para","text":"The cries and pain from this terrible abuse of authority are heard on all borders of the city."},{"kind":"para","text":"The murders didn't just take place in Bethlehem."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's even more miserable..."},{"kind":"para","text":"This king is disgusting."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":23,"title":"The Nazarene","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know how old Herod was when he ordered this, but I hope that the last days of his life were spent in utter misery."},{"kind":"para","text":"It always astounds me that God would keep a man like this alive."},{"kind":"para","text":"God wants people to do whats right."},{"kind":"para","text":"He's not willing that any should perish."},{"kind":"para","text":"A man like this, however, deserves to perish (at least from our personal eyes)."},{"kind":"para","text":"But the reason this is presented here is to prove a few things to the reader."},{"kind":"para","text":"Firstly, it is up to the Lord who dies and who lives."},{"kind":"para","text":"Why He would allow such an evil king to do something so terrible is not for me to know at this time."},{"kind":"para","text":"He doesn't MAKE Herod do this though."},{"kind":"para","text":"Herod has free will just like anyone else."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe the Spirit of God was certainly working in the life of Herod, but Herod rejected it, and the more he fought against God, the more powerless he felt, so he used his authority to kill children which apparently made him feel more powerful."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is important for us to consider, because many leaders today in the world have the same mindset as Herod."},{"kind":"para","text":"Instead of being a servant King (like Jesus will be and has been), people are more inclined to be a ruthless power-hungry king like Herod."},{"kind":"para","text":"They love their titles, more than their responsibilities."},{"kind":"para","text":"They just want the paycheck, the position, and the affirmation, rather than the connection, the service, and the love that is supposed to come with that title."},{"kind":"para","text":"Pastor, to many, means they get to lord over people, manipulate people, use people, and throw people away when they aren't benefitted by them anymore."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's sad, but it's true."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's the same as Herod."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's the same as the Pharisee's we will see throughout this book."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's the same as the religionists all over the world today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Christianity is no longer a life of \"The Way\", it's a life of \"The Play.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"We must act the part, so people think of us as believers, rather than just be who God made us to be, and allow His Spirit to transform us."},{"kind":"para","text":"Churches (mostly) today want to determine how you grow."},{"kind":"para","text":"They take the place of the Holy Spirit so you fit into their mold, rather than you be led and changed by the Lord like Jesus will continuously preach about in the coming chapters."},{"kind":"para","text":"Herod is a prime example of modern church leadership."},{"kind":"para","text":"The only difference is, he is physically hurting people."},{"kind":"para","text":"Church leaders today just spiritually hurt people."},{"kind":"para","text":"It usually results in physical harm, but we can talk about that when we are introduced to the Pharisee's later in this book."},{"kind":"para","text":"For now, Herod is finally dead, and this family has the chance to finally move on from Egypt (a place that symbolizes the world)."}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph is obedient right away."},{"kind":"para","text":"He doesn't wait to find out what will happen if he just stays in Egypt."},{"kind":"para","text":"He goes where he is directed."},{"kind":"para","text":"They don't belong in Egypt."},{"kind":"para","text":"They don't fit with the world."},{"kind":"para","text":"But they had to have safe harbor until they are able to go back to where they belong."},{"kind":"para","text":"The last few years (nearly ten at this point), I have found the necessity to leave the church for a while."},{"kind":"para","text":"I've been to three different churches who were spiritually abusive."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't have the ability to talk in great depths about it in this commentary, but I will say that laying low outside of the church has been great for me and my family's healing."},{"kind":"para","text":"People, of course, frowned upon it."},{"kind":"para","text":"But sometimes, when you are being manipulated, or threatened, or sought after for more abuse, it's best to just stay out of those places for a time."},{"kind":"para","text":"I do believe if someone is truly saved, God will tell you when it's safe to go back."}]},{"start":22,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God leads this family into Nazareth, and yet ANOTHER prophecy is fulfilled."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"","ref":"Isaiah 11:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"Matthew doesn't talk about Jesus being a \"Nazarite\" and taking the vow of a Nazarene."},{"kind":"para","text":"Rather, he just acclaims a nod to the fulfilled prophecy."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus grows up as a Nazarene, but He is not a Nazarite."},{"kind":"para","text":"Splitting hairs here will get me in trouble."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you want to believe He took on a Nazarite vow, by all means feel free to believe it."},{"kind":"para","text":"You'd be hard pressed to explain verses like Him turning water into wine (even drinking from the vine at His last supper), and explaining away verses of touching dead people and bringing the dead back to life."},{"kind":"para","text":"Not that it would be impossible, but your testimony of logistics and apologetics is lost when you are limiting yourself to the law."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's true about this, and it's true about your own faith as well."},{"kind":"para","text":"Faith and salvation doesn't come from the law."},{"kind":"para","text":"Faith and salvation comes from Jesus Christ alone."},{"kind":"para","text":"No law is required."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have no doubt, however, that the prophecies being told in the Old Testament likely didn't make much sense to the people hearing them."},{"kind":"para","text":"It was probably quite confusing how this man can be in four different places at once like this."},{"kind":"para","text":"But when we read the story, it's pretty obvious that it is legitimately possible for this to happen and be true."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":23}]},
  },
};
